        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are portable power stations safe to use indoors?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. A power station has no engine and burns no fuel, so it produces no carbon monoxide and no exhaust of any kind. It is designed for indoor use and is safe in a bedroom, a living room or an apartment — which is the fundamental difference between a battery and a generator.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you run a power station in a bedroom overnight?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, and it is one of the main reasons people buy them, particularly for CPAP machines. Leave a few inches of clearance around the vents so the cooling fan can work, and be aware that the fan may cycle audibly if you are running a heavy load.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do portable power stations produce carbon monoxide?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Carbon monoxide is a product of combustion, and nothing in a power station burns. Only fuel-burning equipment — gasoline, propane, natural gas or diesel generators — produces it, which is why those must never be run indoors or in a garage.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do power stations need ventilation?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They need airflow for cooling rather than ventilation for fumes. Inverters and batteries generate heat under load, and the internal fan needs clear air paths, so keep several inches around the vents and never operate one inside a closed cabinet or under a blanket.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a portable power station catch fire?",
